<h1><%= archived ? "Archived reports" : "Reports" %></h1>

<p class="switch-list">
    <% if archived %>
        <a href="<%= to("/", false) %>">← The working list<%=
            " (#{other})" if other.positive? %></a>
    <% elsif other.positive? %>
        <a href="<%= to("/?archived=1", false) %>">Archived (<%= other %>) →</a>
    <% end %>
</p>

<% if entries.empty? %>
    <p class="empty">
        <% if archived %>
            Nothing archived. The switch in a report’s row — or on
            its own page — takes it out of this list without
            destroying anything.
        <% else %>
            Nothing reported yet. Take the
            <a href="<%= mount("/") %>">bookmarklet</a> to a page and pick
            something.
        <% end %>
    </p>
<% else %>
    <div class="scroller">
        <table class="listing">
            <%# Declared once, for both lists. Left to measure itself,
                each table sized its columns from its own rows: the
                archive — fewer of them, different URLs — gave itself a
                wider Page and a narrower Summary, which wrapped more,
                which made every row taller. Two views of one table that
                did not line up, and a three-row page that scrolled for
                want of a few pixels. %>
            <colgroup>
                <col class="when"><col class="kind"><col class="page">
                <col class="summary"><col class="sent"><col class="by">
                <col class="state"><col class="filed">
            </colgroup>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>When</th><th>Kind</th><th>Page</th>
                    <th>Summary</th><th>Sent</th><th>By</th><th>State</th>
                    <th>Archived</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <% entries.each do |entry| %>
                    <tr>
                        <%# To the minute, without the T and the Z: they
                            are punctuation for a machine, and the width
                            they cost is what the controls at the other
                            end of the row need. The whole stamp is in
                            the title, and in the report. %>
                        <td><a href="<%= to("/#{entry['id']}", false) %>"
                               title="<%= entry["at"] %>"><%=
                            entry["at"].to_s[0, 16].tr("T", " ") %></a></td>
                        <td><%= entry["type"] %></td>
                        <td><%= entry["url"] %></td>
                        <td><%= summarise(entry) %></td>
                        <td><%== channels(entry["channels"]) %></td>
                        <td><%= entry["reporter"] %></td>
                        <%# The state is set from here as well as from
                            the report's own page: triaging a morning's
                            reports meant opening each one and coming
                            back, and the answer is usually obvious from
                            the summary. The list shows the state the
                            report is in, in its colour, so the column
                            still reads down without being opened.

                            `from` is what sends you back here rather
                            than into the report: the same handler, told
                            where the press came from. %>
                        <td class="setting">
                            <form class="pick is-compact" method="post"
                                  action="<%= to("/#{entry['id']}/state", false) %>">
                                <input type="hidden" name="from"
                                       value="<%= archived ? "archive" : "list" %>">
                                <select name="state" aria-label="State">
                                    <% Corrigenda::Store::STATES.each do |value| %>
                                        <option value="<%= value %>"<%=
                                            " selected" if value == entry["state"] %>><%= value %></option>
                                    <% end %>
                                </select>
                                <button>Set</button>
                            </form>
                        </td>

                        <%# The other half of triage, and it used to mean
                            opening the report: the state says what
                            happened to the defect, this says whether
                            anybody still wants it in front of them. A
                            switch, because it is one fact with two
                            values, and its own column, because a
                            control under the states was read as a
                            fourth state. %>
                        <td class="filing">
                            <form method="post"
                                  action="<%= to("/#{entry['id']}/archive", false) %>">
                                <input type="hidden" name="from"
                                       value="<%= archived ? "archive" : "list" %>">
                                <input type="hidden" name="archived"
                                       value="<%= archived ? "0" : "1" %>">
                                <button class="toggle" role="switch"
                                        aria-checked="<%= archived %>"
                                        aria-label="Archived"
                                        title="<%= archived ? "Bring it back to the working list" : "Out of the list, keeping everything" %>"></button>
                            </form>
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                <% end %>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <dl class="legend">
        <% Corrigenda::CHANNELS.each do |key, (mark, label)| %>
            <dt><span class="chip" data-channel="<%= key %>"><%= mark %></span></dt>
            <dd><%= label %></dd>
        <% end %>
    </dl>
<% end %>
